 ## Overview
-Gaussian Mixture Tree (GMT) Construction is a method for organizing 3D Gaussian primitives into a hierarchical tree structure to identify distinct 3D plane instances in a scene [S1]. It solves the problem of spatially scattered Gaussian centers by grouping them into coherent planar surfaces, enabling probabilistic modeling of the scene geometry [S1]. Unlike sibling methods that rely on spatial proximity or feature matching for connectivity [14454-tragraph-gs.md, 22007-surface-aware-splat-graph-construction.md], GMT uses statistical merging criteria based on Bhattacharya distance and plane descriptor similarity to build a multi-level representation from leaf clusters to the root [S1].
+Gaussian Mixture Tree (GMT) Construction is a hierarchical scene representation method that organizes 3D Gaussian primitives into a tree structure to identify distinct 3D plane instances [S1]. Unlike standard 3D Gaussian Splatting which treats primitives as independent entities, GMT recursively merges leaf nodes (local clusters of Gaussians) into parent nodes based on statistical similarity and geometric consistency, enabling plane-aware rendering and deformation [S1]. It is distinguished from sibling methods like [TraGraph-GS](14454-tragraph-gs.md) and [Surface-Aware Splat Graph Construction](22007-surface-aware-splat-graph-construction.md) by its explicit tree hierarchy derived from Gaussian Mixture Models rather than graph adjacency or spatial containment [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs and Outputs**
-*   **Input:** A set of $N_s$ leaf nodes, where each leaf node $G_i$ represents a local cluster of Gaussian primitives. Each node $G_i$ is defined by parameters: mean position $\mu_i \in \mathbb{R}^3$, covariance matrix $\Sigma_i \in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times 3}$, normal vector $n_i \in \mathbb{R}^3$, and a learnable plane descriptor $z_i \in \mathbb{R}^k$ (typically $k=3$) [S1].
-*   **Output:** A Gaussian Mixture Tree $G$ containing parent nodes $G_{s_k}$ and a counter $L$ representing the number of merged plane nodes [S1].
+**Inputs and Outputs:**
+*   **Input:** A set of $N_s$ leaf nodes $\{G_i\}_{i=1}^{N_s}$, where each leaf node $G_i$ represents a local cluster of 3D Gaussian primitives. Each node $G_i$ is defined by mean $\mu_i \in \mathbb{R}^3$, covariance $\Sigma_i \in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times 3}$, normal vector $n_i \in \mathbb{R}^3$, and plane descriptor $z_i \in \mathbb{R}^k$ (typically $k=3$) [S1].
+*   **Output:** A Gaussian Mixture Tree $G$ containing merged parent nodes and a counter $L$ representing the number of plane nodes [S1].
 
